Restore the audio.cpp fork's CUDA pool-trimming and graph-eviction entry points
(ggml_backend_cuda_trim_pools / ggml_backend_cuda_clear_graph), which the engine
resolves dynamically through ggml_backend_reg_get_proc_address in
src/framework/core/backend.cpp.

  - ggml_cuda_pool gains a virtual clear() defaulting to a no-op, so trimming is
    opt-in per pool implementation and never disturbs live allocations: buffers
    already handed out by alloc() are untouched, only idle cached capacity is
    dropped.
  - ggml_backend_cuda_trim_pools synchronizes the device, then clears every
    per-(device, stream) pool.
  - ggml_backend_cuda_clear_graph evicts one cached CUDA graph, keyed by its
    first node, so a rebuilt graph cannot be served from a stale capture. It
    compiles to a no-op when USE_CUDA_GRAPH is off.
  - Both are exported through the reg proc-address table; the engine resolves
    them dynamically, so they are not a hard build dependency.

REGENERATED for ggml 0.22.0 (36da5713). The previous revision of this patch was
hand-written and had never been round-trip verified by a sync run: its third
hunk declared a new-side length of 21 lines for a 35-line body, and the file had
no trailing newline. Both made `git apply` reject it as a corrupt patch, so the
delta was one sync away from being dropped even though its content was present
in the vendored tree. This revision is generated by diff and verified by an
end-to-end scripts/sync-ggml.sh run.

Upstream anchors were re-checked against 36da5713 rather than assumed: the
ggml_cuda_pool interface, ggml_backend_cuda_context::pools
[GGML_CUDA_MAX_DEVICES][GGML_CUDA_MAX_STREAMS], the cuda_graphs map, and the
reg proc-address table all still exist in the shapes this patch needs.

